name: dotnet-channels

Guide to `System.Threading.Channels`: thread-safe producer/consumer queues, back-pressure, `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`, graceful shutdown. For hosted-service lifecycle and `BackgroundService`, see [skill:dotnet-background-services]; for async/cancellation patterns, see [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns].

`Channel<T>` has `ChannelWriter<T>` and `ChannelReader<T>` endpoints; the channel handles synchronization.

```csharp
Channel<WorkItem> channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
ChannelWriter<WorkItem> writer = channel.Writer;
ChannelReader<WorkItem> reader = channel.Reader;
```

Bounded: `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)`; capacity caps memory and `FullMode` controls overflow. Unbounded: `Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>()`; grows without limit and can exhaust memory under load. Use bounded channels for production/untrusted producer rates; unbounded for guaranteed-low-volume workloads or prototyping.

Unbounded -- use only when you control the producer rate.

```csharp
var bounded = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(
    new BoundedChannelOptions(capacity: 1000)
    { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.Wait });
var unbounded = Channel.CreateUnbounded<WorkItem>();
```

`FullMode`: `Wait` (default) blocks `WriteAsync` until space is available; `DropOldest` suits telemetry; `DropNewest` suits rate limiting; `DropWrite` discards the attempted write and makes `TryWrite` return `false`. Since .NET 7, bounded drop modes accept an `itemDropped` callback receiving the dropped item and `ChannelWriter<T>` for metrics, logging, cleanup, or rerouting.

```csharp
var channel = Channel.CreateBounded(new BoundedChannelOptions(100)
{
    FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode.DropOldest
}, itemDropped: (item, writer) =>
{
    logger.LogWarning("Dropped item due to channel overflow: {Id}", item.Id);
    (item as IDisposable)?.Dispose();
});
await writer.WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken);
if (!writer.TryWrite(item)) { /* Handle overflow */ }
```

